transcribe_videos.py transcribes recordings on your own PC using Whisper, an open-source speech-recognition model.

Setup and run

pip install faster-whisper
python transcribe_videos.py "C:\Users\Gagan\Videos\Screen Recordings"

What you get for each recording

A .txt transcript with [hh:mm:ss] timestamps. This is the file to give me.
A .srt subtitle file, so you can play the video with captions.
ALL_TRANSCRIPTS.md, which combines every transcript when you run it on a folder, so I can analyze the whole series in one go.

Options worth knowing

--frames 30 saves a screenshot every 30 seconds. Training sessions are often "click here, then this screen," and the transcript alone misses that. You can attach a few screenshots alongside the transcript.
--model medium is more accurate for jargon-heavy content. small, the default, is quicker on a laptop CPU.
--skip-existing lets you re-run the same folder as you record more sessions, and it only processes the new ones.

Notes

The first run downloads the model once. After that it runs fully offline.
